\section{Make a Parser's Choice Part of Its Type}

A successful parser combinator has an ordinary result type, perhaps an expression tree. Before parsing anything, it also accepts a language with facts that can guide its execution. Can it accept the empty string? Which tokens can start a nonempty match? After an accepted prefix, which tokens could extend that prefix to another accepted string? Krishnaswami and Yallop track these facts as \texttt{Null}, \texttt{First}, and \texttt{FLast} for context-free expressions, then use the type information to implement predictive parsing \cite{krishnaswami2019typedparsing}. This is a static check about the grammar, not inference of the types of parsed expressions.

\section{Find a Boundary That First Sets Alone Miss}

Consider \texttt{a} and \texttt{ab} as two accepted strings of a language \(L\). After a parser has consumed \texttt{a}, \texttt{b} can extend that accepted prefix into another word of \(L\). Thus \(b\) is in \(\mathrm{FLast}(L)\), whereas \(a\) is in \(\mathrm{First}(L)\). In \(L\cdot\{b\}\), the lookahead \texttt{b} at that boundary might extend the left parse or start the right parse. A test that compares only the two first sets would miss this conflict. With a right operand \(\{c\}\), the boundary can be distinguished. To isolate this concatenation check, assume \(L\) is supplied by the factored expression \(\texttt{a}(\epsilon\mid\texttt{b})\), not by a choice between two alternatives that both start with \texttt{a}.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\hline
Expression & Nullable & First & Extension after a word \\
\hline
\(\epsilon\) & yes & empty & empty \\
\(\{b\}\) & no & \(b\) & empty \\
\(\{a,ab\}\) & no & \(a\) & \(b\) \\
\(\{a,ab\}\cdot\{c\}\) & no & \(a\) & empty \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\Cref{fig:visual-382} shows the two uses of the same lookahead at the unsafe boundary. The rejected grammar may still be unambiguous as a specification of complete strings: \texttt{ab} and \texttt{abb} have distinct complete derivations. Rejection says that the stated interface cannot choose the boundary using this predictive rule. A factored equivalent for the accepted words is \(\texttt{ab}(\epsilon\mid\texttt{b})\). If semantic actions distinguished the old decompositions, account for those actions when changing the grammar.

