\section{Reject a Conflicting Composition at Grammar Construction}

The sketch checks two local side conditions on summaries that have already been inferred. It omits the fixed-point rules for recursive grammars and does not by itself calculate the output summary of each composition. The choice rule forbids two distinct empty parses; the sequence rule also requires the left operand not to be nullable in this type system.

\begin{verbatim}
check_choice(p, q):
    conflicts := intersection(p.first, q.first)
    if conflicts not empty:
        report("shared first tokens", conflicts, p.span, q.span)
    if p.nullable and q.nullable:
        report("two empty branches", p.span, q.span)

check_sequence(p, q):
    if p.nullable:
        report("nullable left operand", p.span)
    conflicts := intersection(p.flast, q.first)
    if conflicts not empty:
        report("cannot choose sequence boundary",
               conflicts, p.span, q.span)
\end{verbatim}

For \(\{a,ab\}\cdot\{b\}\) this checker reports \(\{b\}\) and points at both operands. For \(\{a,ab\}\cdot\{c\}\) it finds an empty intersection. Compare the accepted parser's behavior on \texttt{ac}, \texttt{abc}, \texttt{ab}, and \texttt{abbc}, requiring a complete parse with no leftover tokens. The first two are valid; the last two are not. For a reusable combinator, include semantic-action results in the fixtures so recognizing the same token language is not mistaken for preserving the same tree.

\section{Separate a Grammar Diagnostic From an Input Diagnostic}

A conflict between the left and right operands is a problem in the grammar definition. Once that definition is fixed, an ordinary parse error is a problem with a particular input. Keep source locations for grammar operands as well as token locations in the input; the two messages should not refer to the same file by accident. A deterministic decision may improve a diagnostic by letting the parser name the missing token at a known boundary. It does not automatically provide a recovery policy. \Cref{ch:error-recovery} considers what to do after a valid grammar rejects input.

\section{Trace a Nullable Choice Without Backtracking}

Implement the factored left operand with a token parser for \texttt{a}
and a predictive choice between \texttt{b} and \(\epsilon\). The choice
can take the empty arm when the next token is not \texttt{b}. Taking that
arm does not mean the entire input has been accepted. The following
token must still satisfy the right operand and the cursor must be at
end of input when the outer parser returns.

\begin{verbatim}
parse_safe_sequence(input):
    cursor := new_cursor(input)
    a := expect(cursor, 'a')
    optional_b := None
    if lookahead(cursor) == 'b':
        optional_b := Some(expect(cursor, 'b'))
    c := expect(cursor, 'c')
    expect_end(cursor)
    return Sequence(Left(a, optional_b), Right(c))
\end{verbatim}

On \texttt{ac} the lookahead at the choice is \texttt{c}, and the
empty arm leaves it for the second operand. On \texttt{ab} the
lookahead is \texttt{b}, but after consuming it the parser fails
expecting \texttt{c} at end of input. The parser must not reinterpret a
consumed token after this error. Add \texttt{acb} to the test suite:
the operands match a prefix, but the complete parser rejects the
trailing \texttt{b}. If a caller intentionally parses one item from
a larger input, give that entry point a different contract: it
returns the new cursor instead of requiring end of input.

Summaries are allowed to overapproximate actual language properties.
An overly large first or extension set can cause a safe grammar to
be rejected; an undersized set can make the predictive parser choose
wrongly. In a combinator library, construct these summaries from the
grammar operations or validate annotations instead of accepting
arbitrary user-supplied claims about them.
